# Residue — ExactCounts.jl
#
# Obligations the Agda development does NOT discharge, stated so that somebody
# could pick one up cold.  Each entry is something a reader of
# proofs/PROOF-STATUS.md might reasonably assume is proved, and is not.
# Lifted from MetaManifold-WebUI@c7e5a39 proofs/residue/ and re-measured
# against this repository on 2026-10-02.
#
# Format: id, statement, status, why, impact, closes-with.

R-EC-1
  statement: ∀ {A} {r s : Refusal} → Checked.refused {A} r ≡ Checked.refused s → r ≡ s
  status: CLOSED (2026-10-02)
  why: |
    Proved by `refl` once the carrier is explicit (`refused-injective` in
    Counts.agda).  The earlier failure left `A` implicit on the left and the
    unifier blocked on it.  Its use, `checkedAdd-refusal-is-overflow`, is proved.

R-EC-2
  statement: |
    `Fits k` is a (k+1)-bit two's-complement range; that `k = 63` is Julia's
    `Int64` (and 7/15/31 are Int8/16/32) is a fact about the running Julia, not
    about the model.
  status: CHECKED, NOT PROVED (by construction)
  why: A model of Julia's integer widths is out of scope for Agda.
  impact: MEDIUM but bounded — a wrong k would make the guard wrong in a way the proofs accept.
  closes-with: |
    Already mitigated: test/test_proof_vectors.jl "R-EC-2" pins both ends of
    every native signed width with `fits` vectors and checks the overflow guard
    trips exactly past typemax and typemin.  Cannot be closed further on the
    Agda side.

R-EC-3
  statement: |
    `checked_count_sum(...; on_overflow = :widen)` promotes to BigInt instead of
    refusing, and `checked_count_sum` of an EMPTY collection returns 0.
  status: OUT OF SCOPE
  why: |
    `checkedSumOf` models the refusing path over a non-empty collection.  Widening
    has no bound (so no `Fits k` to state), and the empty sum needs no guard.
  impact: LOW.  Both behaviours are unit-tested in test/test_numeric_policy.jl.
  closes-with: |
    An `Outcome`-valued `checkedSum : List (Bounded k) → Checked (Bounded k)`
    with `checkedSum [] ≡ ok 0` and an exactness lemma; :widen stays out.

R-EC-4
  statement: |
    The denominator budget: `exact_fraction`, `exact_relative_abundance` and
    `exact_rational_sum` raise `ResourceLimitError` when a denominator is wider
    than `max_denominator_bits`.
  status: OUT OF SCOPE (Julia-only resource policy)
  why: |
    The model's rationals are unbounded and `relativeAbundance` has no budget
    arm, so nothing proved says when the budget trips or that it is checked
    after the zero-total test.
  impact: |
    LOW for correctness (the budget refuses, it never returns a wrong value),
    but the refusal ORDER past step 3 of docs/numeric-contracts.md is tested,
    not proved.
  closes-with: |
    A `budgetExhausted` Refusal constructor and a bit-width argument to
    `relativeAbundance`, with a lemma that it is reached only after
    `countExceedsTotal` and `zeroTotal` are ruled out.

R-DR-1
  statement: ∀ n d s → ∃ λ m → IsRoundHalfAway n d s m   (existence of the rounding)
  status: CLOSED (2026-10-06)
  closed-by: |
    `exists-halfAway` in ExactCounts/DecimalRounding.agda.  No ℤ division was
    needed (the earlier note here said it was): the sign split in
    `IsRoundHalfAway` reduces n < 0 to its magnitude, and for n = + k the witness
    is the ℕ quotient (2·k·10^s + D) / (2·D), D = d + 1, bounded by
    `m≡m%n+[m/n]*n` and `m%n<n` from Data.Nat.DivMod.  `roundHalfAway` is that
    witness as a function; four `refl` checks show it computes the shipped
    digits, and two gate-selftest mutations show the proof can fail.

R-DR-3
  statement: |
    The integer spec is the rational one: `IsRoundHalfAway n d s m` iff, under
    stdlib ℚ (and ℚᵘ) arithmetic and order, with x = n/(d+1),
    x ≥ 0 ⇒ m/10^s − 1/(2·10^s) ≤ x < m/10^s + 1/(2·10^s), and
    x < 0 ⇒ m/10^s − 1/(2·10^s) < x ≤ m/10^s + 1/(2·10^s).
  status: CLOSED (2026-10-06)
  closed-by: |
    `adequacyᵘ` and `adequacyℚ` in ExactCounts/DecimalRoundingAdequacy.agda.
    The rational statement (`RoundsHalfAwayᵘ`, `RoundsHalfAwayℚ`) is written with
    the library's own `_/_`, `_+_`, `_-_`, `_≤_`, `_<_`, and the sign of x is
    read with the library's order, not from its representation.  Over ℚᵘ each
    bound is cross-multiplication, one scaling by 10^s and one ring identity;
    the negative side negates first.  Over ℚ the order is transferred through
    `toℚᵘ` (`toℚᵘ-mono-≤`/`-cancel-≤`, `toℚᵘ-homo-+`, `toℚᵘ-fromℚᵘ`).
    Corollaries: `roundHalfAway-roundsℚ` (the computed digit rounds correctly
    under ℚ) and `roundsℚ-unique` (it is the only digit that does);
    `neg-tie-roundsℚ` carries −1/8 → −0.13 through the whole chain.  Two
    gate-selftest mutations (negative tie window made non-strict; −0.13 changed
    to −0.12) show the proof can fail.  The original statement named
    `IsRoundHalfUp` and a single window; that is the x ≥ 0 half of this one,
    and the shipped rule is half-away.

R-DR-2
  statement: |
    `to_display` of an APPROXIMATE value uses `Base.round`, which takes ties to
    even (round(0.125; digits = 2) == 0.12), unlike the exact path.
  status: OUT OF SCOPE (by design)
  why: An approximate value has no exact tie to round; the proofs cover exact rationals only.
  impact: LOW; documented in docs/numeric-contracts.md so the two paths are not confused.

R-TC-1
  statement: |
    The proofs are checked against one toolchain: Agda 2.6.4.3 + agda-stdlib 2.1
    (Debian 13 trixie's `agda` and `agda-stdlib` packages).
  status: CLOSED for reproducibility (2026-10-02); portability OPEN
  why: |
    The source repository pinned an UNTAGGED stdlib SHA (2ffa8b7d, the 3.0 line)
    that does not build on any release.  This repository ports the modules to the
    tagged stdlib 2.1, and proofs/lib.sh refuses any other Agda rather than
    trying it.  Not yet checked against stdlib 2.2+ / Agda 2.7+.
  closes-with: A CI matrix row on a newer released pair, when one is adopted.
